<!--
  ============================================================================
  sleela-gui.system.xml — SLeeLa GUI System Definition (BODI™-witnessed)
  ============================================================================

  BODI™ DOCUMENT COMMENT
  ======================
  This document is a BODI™ (Bloq Object Directory Interface) system definition.
  It is a *source artifact*: the SLeeLa toolchain lowers it into two congruent
  outputs — a Java presentation host and a SLeeLa Wrapper™ (.sleela) that owns
  GUI intent. The document does not itself execute; it declares the addressable
  GUI system, its components, its events, and its comparison against the
  Java 28 SecureJDK memory host, so that both emitted forms remain answerable to
  the same witnessed contract.

  Every <container> below sits inside the BODI™ mitigative circumference: each
  addressed change carries system → reference → change → method → sequence →
  starter → man, and every emit is a witnessed BODI™ operation drawn from the
  closed middle-verb set (install/connect/push/enable/open/bind/expose/…).

  BODI™ AUTHORS
  =============
  Author        : Max Rupplin — MEARVK LLC — 2026
  Full name     : Maximilian Eric Alexander Rupplin von Keffikon
  Witness (man) : operator
  Starter       : gui
  Trademarks    : BODI™ (Bloq Object Directory Interface); Wrapper™ (.sleela).
  Governance    : SL-META-0001 (Sleela Language Metadocument); BODI.md;
                  gui/README.md + gui/INTEGRATION.md; java28/spec/
                  JAVA28-MEMORY-INTEGRATION.md (series J28-MEM-0001).

  BODI™ SYSTEM PREFACE
  ====================
  The SLeeLa GUI system is intentionally asymmetric. SLeeLa owns the *meaning*
  of the interface — windows requested, text set, named actions bound, events
  interpreted — while Java owns *presentation* (Swing / JavaFX) and the desktop
  lifecycle. Neither side is a copy of the other: Java is a native desktop host
  for SLeeLa, not a second business-logic implementation.

  This preface fixes the reading order for everything that follows:

    1. A GUI is an ADDRESSED system. Its BODI™ address is
       Bodi.system("sleela.gui"). Every component and event resolves under
       that address; the registry reference is urn:sleela:bodi:1.
    2. INTENT precedes MECHANISM. The SLeeLa surface states *what* the UI means;
       the Java backend decides *how* it is realized (a JFrame or a Stage).
    3. WITNESS precedes CHANGE. No component is created, shown, or bound without
       a BODI™ witness recording the operation on the circumference.
    4. The Java 28 SecureJDK is the MEMORY host, never the logic host. When the
       GUI needs durable object state it drives Java-resident objects through
       opaque handles over the J28-MEM-0001 value contract — the same contract
       whether the link is JNI (local feedback) or a loopback port.
    5. This document is the single source of truth for BOTH emitted forms. If a
       detail is absent here it must not be invented in Java or in SLeeLa; the
       emit stops at the boundary (BODI™ `contain`) rather than guess.

  The body is organized as three BODI™ containers keyed by lifecycle mode:
    mode="1" purpose="startup"   — declare + emit the GUI system (compile time)
    mode="2" purpose="runtime"   — the live event / intent circumference
    mode="3" purpose="shutdown"  — orderly teardown and witness close
  ============================================================================
-->

<document
    xmlns:bodi="urn:sleela:bodi:1"
    system="sleela.gui"
    version="1.0"
    sleela-syntax="1.2"
    conduct-frame="US">

    <!-- ====================================================================
         BODI™ system identity + author block (machine-readable mirror of the
         document comment above). The <bodi:witness> address is the stable
         semantic handle every emitted artifact resolves against.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <preface title="SLeeLa GUI System — BODI™ System Preface">
        <trademark>BODI™ — Bloq Object Directory Interface</trademark>
        <trademark>Wrapper™ — the .sleela source filetype</trademark>
        <author role="attention">Max Rupplin — MEARVK LLC — 2026</author>
        <author role="full-name">Maximilian Eric Alexander Rupplin von Keffikon</author>
        <bodi:witness
            system="sleela.gui"
            reference="urn:sleela:bodi:1"
            sequence="001"
            starter="gui"
            man="operator" />
        <statement>
            SLeeLa owns GUI intent and event meaning; Java owns Swing/JavaFX
            presentation and the desktop lifecycle; the Java 28 SecureJDK owns
            durable object memory reached by opaque handle. This XML is the one
            source both emitted forms are answerable to.
        </statement>
    </preface>

    <!-- ====================================================================
         COMPILATION CONTRACT — how this XML becomes Java + SLeeLa.
         The toolchain reads this system document and emits two congruent forms.
         Each <emit> is a witnessed BODI™ operation (verb from the closed set).
         ==================================================================== -->
    <compilation model="1" purpose="xml-to-java-and-sleela">

        <!-- Emit 1: the SLeeLa Wrapper™ that owns GUI intent (Path 2). -->
        <emit target="sleela" verb="install" artifact="SleelaGui.sleela">
            <bodi:change
                system="sleela.gui" method="install" sequence="010"
                starter="compiler" man="operator" />
            <pragma>#sleela 1.2</pragma>
            <role>
                A Wrapper™ that expresses window/text/action intent and binds
                named operations. Lowered to core bytecode by the Sleela front
                end (COMPILER.md); it drives presentation through the runtime
                boundary, never touching a toolkit widget directly.
            </role>
            <maps-to kind="sleela-class">com.mearvk.sleela.gui (intent surface)</maps-to>
        </emit>

        <!-- Emit 2: the Java presentation host + backends (Path 1 + Path 3). -->
        <emit target="java" verb="bind" artifact="com.mearvk.sleela.gui">
            <bodi:change
                system="sleela.gui" method="bind" sequence="011"
                starter="compiler" man="operator" />
            <language-level>Java 28 SecureJDK (source/target; forward-compatible)</language-level>
            <role>
                The toolkit-neutral contract (SleelaGui) plus the Swing and
                JavaFX backends, the host adapters, and the native bridge facade.
                Java is the desktop host; it defers all business meaning to
                SLeeLa through SleelaRuntime.
            </role>
            <maps-to kind="java-package">com.mearvk.sleela.gui</maps-to>
        </emit>

        <!-- The two emits MUST be congruent: same components, same events, same
             action names. Divergence is a BODI™ boundary stop (contain). -->
        <congruence verb="observe" rule="strict">
            Component ids, event names, action names, and the document-change
            listener option (including its 1..14 bound) are identical across the
            Java and SLeeLa emits. A name present in one and absent in the other
            is rejected at compile time (BODI™ `contain`), never bridged.
        </congruence>
    </compilation>

    <!-- ====================================================================
         MODE 1 — STARTUP: declare the GUI system and its components.
         This is the compile-time surface: the shape both forms are emitted from.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <container mode="1" purpose="startup">

        <guard>

            <!-- The addressed GUI system. class= is the Java contract type;
                 wrapper= is the SLeeLa intent surface. Both are emitted. -->
            <container
                class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.SleelaGui"
                wrapper="sleela.gui.SleelaGui"
                interface="AutoCloseable">

                <bodi:reference address="urn:sleela:bodi:1" system="sleela.gui" />

                <!-- ============================================================
                     TOOLKIT-NEUTRAL CONTRACT (SleelaGui)
                     The single operation set both backends implement and both
                     emitted forms speak. Each operation is a BODI™ verb.
                     ============================================================ -->
                <operations>
                    <operation name="show" verb="open" returns="void">
                        <param name="title"  type="String" sl-type="String" />
                        <param name="width"  type="int"    sl-type="int" />
                        <param name="height" type="int"    sl-type="int" />
                        <intent>Open a top-level window of the requested size and title.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="setText" verb="push" returns="void">
                        <param name="text" type="String" sl-type="String" />
                        <intent>Publish text into the window's primary label.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="onAction" verb="bind" returns="void">
                        <param name="action" type="Runnable" sl-type="action" />
                        <intent>Bind a callback to the window's primary action control.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="refresh" verb="push" returns="void" default="true">
                        <param name="documentId" type="String" sl-type="String" />
                        <param name="revision"   type="String" sl-type="String" />
                        <intent>Refresh the running GUI for a changed watched document; marshaled onto the toolkit thread by the backend.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="close" verb="close" returns="void">
                        <intent>Dispose the window and release toolkit resources.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="closeQuietly" verb="contain" returns="void" default="true">
                        <intent>Deterministic host-shutdown close that swallows runtime faults.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="create" verb="activate" returns="SleelaGui" static="true">
                        <param name="backend" type="String" sl-type="String" />
                        <intent>Factory: select the "swing" or "javafx"/"fx" backend.</intent>
                    </operation>
                </operations>

                <!-- ============================================================
                     COMPONENT MODEL — toolkit-neutral, with the concrete widget
                     each backend realizes it as. This is the "all the details of
                     GUI" surface: window, label, action button, layout, thread.
                     ============================================================ -->
                <components>
                    <component id="window" role="top-level" verb="open">
                        <swing widget="javax.swing.JFrame"
                               close-op="DISPOSE_ON_CLOSE"
                               placement="setLocationByPlatform(true)" />
                        <javafx widget="javafx.stage.Stage" scene="javafx.scene.Scene" />
                        <bodi:witness system="sleela.gui" sequence="101"
                                      starter="gui" man="operator" />
                    </component>

                    <component id="content" role="container" verb="mount">
                        <swing widget="javax.swing.JPanel" layout="java.awt.BorderLayout" />
                        <javafx widget="javafx.scene.layout.VBox" spacing="10.0" />
                    </component>

                    <component id="label" role="display" verb="push" initial-text="SLeeLa">
                        <swing widget="javax.swing.JLabel" align="JLabel.CENTER"
                               region="BorderLayout.CENTER" />
                        <javafx widget="javafx.scene.control.Label" region="VBox[0]" />
                    </component>

                    <component id="action" role="command" verb="bind" caption="Action">
                        <swing widget="javax.swing.JButton" region="BorderLayout.SOUTH"
                               listener="addActionListener" />
                        <javafx widget="javafx.scene.control.Button" region="VBox[1]"
                                listener="setOnAction" />
                    </component>

                    <!-- The watched-document surface. Not a widget: it is the set
                         of 1..14 documents the listen option observes; a change
                         drives the `refresh` operation on the live window. -->
                    <component id="document" role="observed-source" verb="observe"
                               min="1" max="14"
                               model="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.SleelaDocument">
                        <field name="id"       type="String" note="stable SLeeLa-facing name" />
                        <field name="path"     type="java.nio.file.Path" note="resolved host path" />
                        <field name="revision" type="String"
                               note="OS-derived fingerprint: lastModifiedMillis:size ('absent' if gone)" />
                        <bodi:witness system="sleela.gui" sequence="102"
                                      starter="gui" man="operator" />
                    </component>
                </components>

                <!-- ============================================================
                     EVENT MODEL — how a toolkit event becomes a SLeeLa-witnessed
                     named action. Thread-affinity is part of the contract.
                     ============================================================ -->
                <events>
                    <event id="action-fired" source="action" verb="observe">
                        <swing origin="java.awt.event.ActionEvent" />
                        <javafx origin="javafx.event.ActionEvent" />
                        <dispatch>Runnable.run() → SleelaGuiRuntime action name → SLeeLa</dispatch>
                        <bodi:witness system="sleela.gui" sequence="201"
                                      starter="gui" man="operator" />
                    </event>
                    <event id="window-closing" source="window" verb="close">
                        <swing origin="WindowEvent (DISPOSE_ON_CLOSE)" />
                        <javafx origin="Stage.close()" />
                    </event>

                    <!-- Document change: raised by the OS watch (or refreshNow),
                         not by a toolkit control. It flows to SleelaGui.refresh()
                         on the toolkit thread and updates the running window. -->
                    <event id="document-changed" source="document" verb="observe">
                        <origin os="linux"   facility="inotify" />
                        <origin os="windows" facility="ReadDirectoryChangesW" />
                        <origin os="macos"   facility="kqueue / FSEvents" />
                        <dispatch>WatchService OS call → DocumentListener.Change → SleelaGui.refresh(id, revision)</dispatch>
                        <bodi:witness system="sleela.gui" sequence="202"
                                      starter="gui" man="operator" />
                    </event>
                </events>

                <!-- ============================================================
                     THREAD CONTRACT — every mutation runs on the toolkit thread.
                     The emitted backends MUST honor this; SLeeLa intent is
                     marshaled onto it. This is a hard part of "GUI detail."
                     ============================================================ -->
                <threading verb="enable">
                    <swing thread="Event Dispatch Thread"
                           guard="javax.swing.SwingUtilities.isEventDispatchThread()"
                           marshal="SwingUtilities.invokeLater" />
                    <javafx thread="JavaFX Application Thread"
                            guard="javafx.application.Platform.isFxApplicationThread()"
                            marshal="javafx.application.Platform.runLater" />
                    <rule>
                        No component field is read or written off the toolkit
                        thread. A null component (pre-show / post-close) is a
                        no-op, never a fault.
                    </rule>
                </threading>

                <!-- ============================================================
                     BACKENDS — the two concrete SleelaGui implementations.
                     ============================================================ -->
                <backends>
                    <backend id="swing" class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.SwingGui"
                             selector="swing" verb="activate" />
                    <backend id="javafx" class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.FxGui"
                             selector="javafx|fx" verb="activate" />
                </backends>

                <!-- ============================================================
                     DOCUMENT-CHANGE LISTENER OPTION
                     SLeeLa's option to listen to document changes over 1..14
                     documents. When a watched document changes, the running GUI
                     is updated; detection is driven by the OS (refresh on OS
                     call), never by content polling. Both emitted forms speak
                     the same option, so the SLeeLa Wrapper™ and the Java host
                     enable it identically.
                     ============================================================ -->
                <document-listener class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.DocumentListener"
                                   verb="observe" default="off">

                    <bodi:reference address="urn:sleela:bodi:1" system="sleela.gui.documents" />

                    <!-- The hard 1..14 document-count contract. -->
                    <bound min="1" max="14"
                           enforced-at="construction and watch()"
                           on-violation="IllegalArgumentException (BODI™ contain)" />

                    <!-- Refresh on OS call(s): the watch blocks in an OS call and
                         wakes only when the OS reports activity; each wake, plus
                         any explicit refreshNow(), re-evaluates the documents. -->
                    <os-refresh mechanism="java.nio.file.WatchService">
                        <call os="linux"   facility="inotify"
                              blocking-call="WatchService.poll" />
                        <call os="windows" facility="ReadDirectoryChangesW"
                              blocking-call="WatchService.poll" />
                        <call os="macos"   facility="kqueue / FSEvents"
                              blocking-call="WatchService.poll" />
                        <coalesce note="OS events are a wake signal only; the real
                                        change is confirmed by re-reading each
                                        document's OS revision (lastModified:size)." />
                        <explicit method="refreshNow"
                                  note="A host may force a refresh from an explicit OS/lifecycle callback; returns the number of documents that changed." />
                    </os-refresh>

                    <!-- The listener's own operation surface. -->
                    <operations>
                        <operation name="watch" verb="start" returns="void">
                            <intent>Register each document's parent dir with the OS WatchService and start the watch thread.</intent>
                        </operation>
                        <operation name="refreshNow" verb="observe" returns="int">
                            <intent>Re-evaluate all watched documents now; emit a change per moved revision; return the count.</intent>
                        </operation>
                        <operation name="watchedIds" verb="observe" returns="List&lt;String&gt;">
                            <intent>The watched document ids, in insertion order.</intent>
                        </operation>
                        <operation name="isRunning" verb="observe" returns="boolean" />
                        <operation name="close" verb="stop" returns="void">
                            <intent>Stop the watch thread and release the OS watch handle. Idempotent.</intent>
                        </operation>
                    </operations>

                    <!-- The single change record delivered per detected change. -->
                    <change model="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.DocumentListener.Change">
                        <field name="previous" type="SleelaDocument" />
                        <field name="current"  type="SleelaDocument" />
                        <field name="id"       type="String" />
                    </change>

                    <!-- End to end: OS call → confirmed change → live GUI update. -->
                    <flow>OS notification (WatchService.poll) → confirm revision → DocumentListener.Change → SleelaGui.refresh(id, revision) on the toolkit thread → running window updated</flow>

                    <boundary verb="contain">
                        Fewer than 1 or more than 14 documents is rejected. A
                        watch event that does not move a document's OS revision is
                        coalesced away (no GUI update). A faulty refresh callback
                        never stops the listener; the document stays observable.
                    </boundary>
                </document-listener>

            </container>

        </guard>

        <!-- ====================================================================
             INTEGRATION PATHS — the three ways SLeeLa and Java compose
             (gui/INTEGRATION.md). Each is a BODI™ connect relationship.
             ==================================================================== -->
        <integration>

            <path id="1" name="java-host-to-sleela-logic" verb="connect" deploy="today">
                <flow>Java GUI → SleelaGuiHost → SleelaRuntime → SLeeLa → slcore_exchange()</flow>
                <adapter class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.SleelaGuiHost">
                    <ctor>
                        <param name="gui"     type="SleelaGui" />
                        <param name="runtime" type="SleelaRuntime" />
                    </ctor>
                    <operation name="show" verb="open">
                        <param name="title"  type="String" />
                        <param name="width"  type="int" />
                        <param name="height" type="int" />
                        <param name="actionOperation" type="String" />
                        <intent>Show a window and route its action to a SLeeLa operation.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="listen" verb="observe" returns="DocumentListener">
                        <param name="idToPath"        type="Map&lt;String,Path&gt;" />
                        <param name="changeOperation" type="String" />
                        <intent>Listen to 1..14 documents; on each OS-detected change call the SLeeLa operation and refresh the running window with its result.</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="refreshNow" verb="observe" returns="int"
                               intent="Force an explicit OS/lifecycle refresh of the watched documents." />
                    <operation name="stopListening" verb="stop" returns="void" />
                    <operation name="close" verb="close" />
                </adapter>
                <adapter class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.ProcessSleelaRuntime"
                         role="invokes native SLeeLa executable" />
                <runtime interface="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.SleelaRuntime">
                    <operation name="call" verb="push" returns="Object">
                        <param name="operation" type="String" />
                        <param name="arguments" type="Object..." />
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="close" verb="close" />
                    <operation name="unsupported" verb="reject" static="true"
                               returns="SleelaRuntime"
                               intent="Null binding that rejects every call." />
                </runtime>
                <use>Java is the launcher; SLeeLa remains the business-logic authority.</use>
            </path>

            <path id="2" name="sleela-intent-to-java-gui" verb="connect" deploy="wrapper-native">
                <flow>SLeeLa program → GUI intent → SleelaGuiRuntime → Swing / JavaFX</flow>
                <adapter class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.SleelaGuiRuntime">
                    <ctor><param name="gui" type="SleelaGui" /></ctor>
                    <operation name="window" verb="open">
                        <param name="title"  type="String" />
                        <param name="width"  type="int" />
                        <param name="height" type="int" />
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="text" verb="push">
                        <param name="text" type="String" />
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="action" verb="bind">
                        <param name="name"      type="String" />
                        <param name="operation" type="Runnable" />
                        <intent>Register a named action; dispatch resolves by name (LinkedHashMap order).</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="listen" verb="observe" returns="DocumentListener">
                        <param name="idToPath" type="Map&lt;String,Path&gt;" />
                        <intent>Listen to 1..14 documents; on each OS-detected change refresh the running window via SleelaGui.refresh(id, revision).</intent>
                    </operation>
                    <operation name="refreshNow" verb="observe" returns="int"
                               intent="Force an explicit OS/lifecycle refresh; 0 when not listening." />
                    <operation name="isListening" verb="observe" returns="boolean" />
                    <operation name="stopListening" verb="stop" returns="void" />
                    <operation name="close" verb="close" />
                </adapter>
                <use>The Wrapper™ describes UI behavior; Java is a presentation substrate.</use>
            </path>

            <path id="3" name="shared-native-abi" verb="connect" deploy="high-performance">
                <flow>SLeeLa → C/C++ runtime → slgui_bridge → JNI → Swing / JavaFX</flow>
                <adapter native-header="gui/native/sleela_gui_bridge.h"
                         native-source="gui/native/sleela_gui_bridge.cpp"
                         boundary="C-compatible callback">
                    <refresh-hook type="SLGuiRefreshFn"
                                  install="slgui_bridge_on_document_change"
                                  deliver="slgui_bridge_document_changed"
                                  signature="void (*)(void *context, const char *document_id, const char *revision)"
                                  bound-macros="SLGUI_MIN_DOCUMENTS=1 SLGUI_MAX_DOCUMENTS=14"
                                  intent="Native counterpart of DocumentListener → SleelaGui.refresh(): the C/C++ side reports a watched-document change so the hosted GUI refreshes." />
                </adapter>
                <adapter class="com.mearvk.sleela.gui.JniSleelaRuntime"
                         role="Java facade for direct native/JNI binding" />
                <use>Preferred when a direct VM connection / low overhead is required.</use>
            </path>

        </integration>

    </container>

    <!-- ====================================================================
         MODE 2 — RUNTIME: the live circumference. GUI events are witnessed
         and, when durable object state is needed, driven against the
         Java 28 SecureJDK memory host over the J28-MEM-0001 value contract.

         THIS IS THE "GUI COMPARED TO JAVA 28" SURFACE: the GUI never holds a
         Java object — it holds an opaque handle and drives it by value.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <container mode="2" purpose="runtime">

        <!-- The Java 28 SecureJDK comparison / memory link (series J28-MEM-0001).
             Model A: SLeeLa drives; Java 28 owns the object memory. -->
        <java28 model="A" series="J28-MEM-0001" host="Java 28 SecureJDK" verb="connect">

            <bodi:reference address="urn:sleela:bodi:1" system="sleela.gui.memory" />

            <roles>
                <role who="driver" owner="SLeeLa (C/C++ core)"
                      holds="handles, never objects" />
                <role who="memory-host" owner="Java 28 SecureJDK"
                      owns="every object on its heap; assigns handles" />
                <role who="link" owner="JNI or port"
                      carries="operations and values" />
            </roles>

            <!-- Value contract: SLValue ↔ Java 28. GUI text/sizes/flags cross
                 here as values; GUI-bound domain objects cross as handles. -->
            <value-contract>
                <value sl="SL_NULL"   wire="n" java="null" />
                <value sl="SL_INT"    wire="i" java="long" />
                <value sl="SL_DOUBLE" wire="d" java="double" />
                <value sl="SL_BOOL"   wire="b" java="boolean" />
                <value sl="SL_STR"    wire="s" java="String (UTF-8)" />
                <value sl="handle"    wire="h" java="live object in the host handle table" />
                <handle-rules>
                    <rule>A handle is a positive int64; 0 is the null handle.</rule>
                    <rule>Handles are opaque to SLeeLa — stored/passed/returned, never interpreted.</rule>
                    <rule>A handle is valid only for its originating session; never persisted.</rule>
                    <rule>The host owns object lifetime; SLeeLa releases interest with `free`.</rule>
                </handle-rules>
            </value-contract>

            <!-- Operation set — identical over both channels. -->
            <operation-set>
                <op name="hello"    verb="open"     request="protocol/version handshake" reply="server identity + version" />
                <op name="new"      verb="install"  request="class name, ctor args"       reply="object handle" />
                <op name="call"     verb="push"     request="handle, method, args"        reply="value or handle" />
                <op name="get"      verb="pull"     request="handle, field"               reply="value or handle" />
                <op name="set"      verb="push"     request="handle, field, value"        reply="ok" />
                <op name="free"     verb="uninstall" request="handle"                     reply="ok" />
                <op name="describe" verb="observe"  request="handle"                      reply="class + field/method summary" />
                <op name="stats"    verb="observe"  request="—"                           reply="live handle count, high-water mark" />
                <op name="bye"      verb="close"    request="—"                           reply="closes the session" />
            </operation-set>

            <!-- The two interchangeable links. Same grammar; same dispatcher. -->
            <channels dispatcher="SleelaMemoryServer.handleLine">
                <channel id="jni" name="local feedback" verb="attach" latency="lowest"
                         entry="const char *sl_java28_call(void *session, const char *request_line)"
                         java-facade="com.mearvk.sleela.java28.Java28JniRuntime"
                         native="java28/src/native/sleela_java28_bridge.c" />
                <channel id="port" name="socket" verb="connect" isolation="process"
                         endpoint="127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;"
                         framing="UTF-8, newline-delimited, length-prefixed strings"
                         java-server="com.mearvk.sleela.java28.Java28PortServer"
                         native="java28/src/native/sleela_java28_port.c" />
            </channels>

            <!-- Security posture inherited by the GUI when it drives Java 28. -->
            <security posture="SecureJDK" verb="approve">
                <control>No arbitrary classes: `new`/`call` restricted to a host allow-list (registerClass).</control>
                <control>Handle isolation: a session may only touch handles it was given; stale/forged → ERR.</control>
                <control>No DTD / no code loading over the wire: values and names only, never bytecode.</control>
                <control>Bounded memory: a maximum live-handle count; exceeding it fails `new`, not the heap.</control>
            </security>

